[1mNAME[0m
       pcap - Packet Capture library

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1m#include <pcap/pcap.h>[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       The  Packet  Capture  library provides a high level interface to packet
       capture systems. All packets on the network, even  those  destined  for
       other  hosts,  are accessible through this mechanism.  It also supports
       saving captured packets to a ``savefile'', and reading packets  from  a
       ``savefile''.

   [1mInitializing[0m
       [1mpcap_init[22m()  initializes  the library.  It takes an argument giving op‐
       tions; currently, the options are:

       [1mPCAP_CHAR_ENC_LOCAL[0m
              Treat all strings supplied as arguments, and return all  strings
              to the caller, as being in the local character encoding.

       [1mPCAP_CHAR_ENC_UTF_8[0m
              Treat  all strings supplied as arguments, and return all strings
              to the caller, as being in UTF-8.

       On UNIX-like systems, the local character encoding  is  assumed  to  be
       UTF-8, so no character encoding transformations are done.

       On Windows, the local character encoding is the local ANSI code page.

       If  [1mpcap_init[22m()  is called, the deprecated [1mpcap_lookupdev[22m() routine al‐
       ways fails, so it should not be used, and,  on  Windows,  [1mpcap_create[22m()
       does not attempt to handle UTF-16LE strings.

       If [1mpcap_init[22m() is not called, strings are treated as being in the local
       ANSI  code page on Windows, [1mpcap_lookupdev[22m() will succeed if there is a
       device on which to capture, and [1mpcap_create[22m() makes an attempt to check
       whether the string passed as an argument is a UTF-16LE  string  -  note
       that this attempt is unsafe, as it may run past the end of the string -
       to  handle  [1mpcap_lookupdev[22m() returning a UTF-16LE string. Programs that
       don't call [1mpcap_init[22m() should, on  Windows,  call  [1mpcap_wsockinit[22m()  to
       initialize  Winsock; this is not necessary if [1mpcap_init[22m() is called, as
       [1mpcap_init[22m() will initialize Winsock itself on Windows.

