   [1mReading packets[0m
       Packets are read with [1mpcap_dispatch[22m() or [1mpcap_loop[22m(), which process one
       or more packets, calling a callback routine for each  packet,  or  with
       [1mpcap_next[22m() or [1mpcap_next_ex[22m(), which return the next packet.  The call‐
       back  for  [1mpcap_dispatch[22m()  and  [1mpcap_loop[22m() is supplied a pointer to a
       [4mstruct[24m [4mpcap_pkthdr[24m, which includes the following members:

              [1mts     [22ma [4mstruct[24m [4mtimeval[24m containing the time when the packet  was
                     captured

              [1mcaplen [22ma  [4mbpf_u_int32[24m  giving  the number of bytes of the packet
                     that are available from the capture

              [1mlen    [22ma [4mbpf_u_int32[24m giving the length of the packet,  in  bytes
                     (which  might  be more than the number of bytes available
                     from the capture, if the length of the packet  is  larger
                     than the maximum number of bytes to capture).

       The  callback  is  also  supplied  a  [4mconst[24m [4mu_char[24m pointer to the first
       [1mcaplen [22m(as given in the [4mstruct[24m [4mpcap_pkthdr[24m mentioned  above)  bytes  of
       data  from the packet.  This won't necessarily be the entire packet; to
       capture the entire packet, you will have to provide a value for [4msnaplen[0m
       in your call to [1mpcap_set_snaplen[22m() that is sufficiently  large  to  get
       all  of  the  packet's  data - a value of 65535 should be sufficient on
       most if not all networks).  When reading from a ``savefile'', the snap‐
       shot length specified when the capture was  performed  will  limit  the
       amount of packet data available.

       [1mpcap_next[22m()  is  passed an argument that points to a [4mstruct[24m [4mpcap_pkthdr[0m
       structure, and fills it in with the time stamp and  length  values  for
       the packet.  It returns a [4mconst[24m [4mu_char[24m to the first [1mcaplen [22mbytes of the
       packet on success, and [1mNULL [22mon error.

       [1mpcap_next_ex[22m()  is passed two pointer arguments, one of which points to
       a [4mstruct[24mpcap_pkthdr[4m*[24m and one of which points to a  [4mconst[24m  [4mu_char[24m*.   It
       sets  the first pointer to point to a [4mstruct[24m [4mpcap_pkthdr[24m structure with
       the time stamp and length values for the packet, and  sets  the  second
       pointer to point to the first [1mcaplen [22mbytes of the packet.

       To  force the loop in [1mpcap_dispatch[22m() or [1mpcap_loop[22m() to terminate, call
       [1mpcap_breakloop[22m().

       By default, when reading packets from an interface opened  for  a  live
       capture,  [1mpcap_dispatch[22m(),  [1mpcap_next[22m(), and [1mpcap_next_ex[22m() will, if no
       packets are currently available to be read, block waiting  for  packets
       to  become  available.   On  some,  but [4mnot[24m all, platforms, if a packet
       buffer timeout was specified, the wait will terminate after the  packet
       buffer timeout expires; applications should be prepared for this, as it
       happens  on  some  platforms, but should not rely on it, as it does not
       happen on other platforms.  Note that the wait  might,  or  might  not,
       terminate even if no packets are available; applications should be pre‐
       pared for this to happen, but must not rely on it happening.

       A  handle can be put into ``non-blocking mode'', so that those routines
       will, rather than blocking, return an indication that  no  packets  are
       available  to  read.  Call [1mpcap_setnonblock[22m() to put a handle into non-
       blocking mode or to take it out of non-blocking mode; call [1mpcap_getnon‐[0m
       [1mblock[22m() to determine whether a handle is in  non-blocking  mode.   Note
       that non-blocking mode does not work correctly in Mac OS X 10.6.

       Non-blocking  mode is often combined with routines such as [1mselect[22m(2) or
       [1mpoll[22m(2) or other routines a platform offers to wait for any of a set of
